ich have demonstrated both efficacy and a favorable safety profile. [Data: Entities (210), Relationships (304)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Galcanezumab as a monoclonal antibody targeting CGRP ligand\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Fremanezumab is a humanized monoclonal antibody approved for the preventive treatment of both episodic and chronic migraine. It works by binding directly to the CGRP ligand, thereby inhibiting its activity and preventing migraine onset. The drug is administered via subcutaneous injection and offers flexible dosing regimens—either monthly or every three months—making it a convenient option for long-term management. Its efficacy and safety are well-established through multiple randomized controlled trials. The most commonly reported adverse reactions are injection site reactions, which are generally mild and manageable. [Data: Entities (209), Relationships (303)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"Fremanezumab as a flexible-dose monoclonal antibody for migraine prevention\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) plays a critical regulatory role in this community by approving key migraine treatments such as rimegepant and atogepant for preventive use. The FDA’s approval process ensures that these medications meet rigorous standards for safety, efficacy, and quality. The fact that both rimegepant and atogepant have received FDA approval underscores their clinical importance and reliability in migraine care. The FDA’s involvement also provides a level of public trust and standardization in the use of these therapies. [Data: Entities (222), Relationships (318)]\",\n            \"summary\": \"FDA's role in approving and validating migraine therapeutics\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"explanation\": \"Ubrogepant is a small molecule oral CGRP receptor antagonist that has been studied and shown to be effective in relieving acute migraine pain. It is classifi